Plan the task:
1. Resolve the implicit meaning of the instruction and disambiguate it. Example: "it is raining" means waterproof items are needed; "an appropriate jacket" then means a rain jacket.
2. List the concrete, physically detectable target objects the robot must find to fulfil the request (each one a single object the robot can stand next to). Think about what is needed to act, not only the objects named: to throw away a cup -> a trash can; to rest -> a sofa or an armchair; to print -> a printer; to wash hands -> a sink.{% if move %} Objects named only as landmarks of a route (rule 6) are not targets.{% endif %}
3. For each target give 1 to 3 detector_prompts: short noun phrases of 1 to 3 words for an open-vocabulary object detector, including one broader fallback class (for "rain boots": ["rain boots", "boots"]). The detector cannot see rooms or abstract things: if the goal is a room ("find the living room"), use objects that indicate it (e.g. sofa, TV) as targets.
4. Optionally give room_priors: room types where each target is likely.
5. Plan atomic subtasks in an order that reduces travel. Use {"type": "goto", "target": <name>, "node_id": <id>} only when a remembered object #id plausibly IS that target; otherwise {"type": "explore", "target": <name>, "room_hint": <room type or null>}.{% if move %}
6. Routes: a part of the instruction that describes a route or a place relative to the robot (follow the corridor, go through the door, turn left at the plant, stop at the end of the hall) is ONE {"type": "move", "target": "<short label, e.g. corridor end>", "instruction": "<that whole route as one imperative sentence>"}. Objects named only to describe a route or where it ends (the sofa to walk past, the plant to stop near, the wall to follow) are landmarks inside the move instruction, NOT targets: list a target only for an object the user wants found, reached or used. Plan every part of the instruction in its order, e.g. "find a printer, then walk to the door" -> explore printer, then move; "go down the hall, then find a sofa" -> move, then explore sofa. An instruction that is only a route has no targets; an instruction without a route has no move.{% endif %}

Return only JSON:
{"reasoning": "<short>", "targets": [{"name": "<target>", "detector_prompts": ["<phrase>"], "room_priors": ["<room type>"]}], "subtasks": [{"type": "explore", "target": "<target>", "node_id": null, "room_hint": null}{% if move %}, {"type": "move", "target": "<short label>", "instruction": "<route>", "node_id": null, "room_hint": null} (only for a route){% endif %}]}
